Definitions in plain language:
- Clipping ratio (α): factor that scales the max absolute value used as the quantization range: threshold T = α · max|x|. Values above T are saturated ("clipped") to the nearest quantization level. α=1.0 means "no clipping beyond the observed max".
- Symmetric quantization: zero point fixed at 0, range [-T, T]. Plain: the scale is centered on zero.
- Asymmetric quantization (KV cache): min/max affine mapping with a zero point, used because K/V activations are offset/biased.
- Perplexity: exp(mean cross-entropy per token); lower is better.

Sweep: fixed scalar α shared by input and KV quantizers, grid α ∈ {1.00, 0.98, 0.96, 0.94, 0.92, 0.90, 0.88, 0.85, 0.80, 0.75, 0.70} — coarse-then-fine (two-stage: coarse as above; refine around argmin with step 0.005–0.01 in ±0.02). Granularity: global per-tensor as primary (the fixed brief says fixed clipping ratio); report per-layer and per-head variants as secondary ablation only if budget permits. Actually the question asks "optimal fixed clipping ratio" — so global scalar primary; per-layer/per-head as secondary analysis to explain why a single global α works/fails.

